<Document
  title="Privacy"
  description="What J5 Code sends from your machines, who receives it, and how to turn it off."
  updated="2026-10-04"
>
  <p>
    J5 Code is open-source software maintained by Jackson Miller. It runs on your own machines.
    Your projects, conversations, and files stay there; J5 Code has no hosted service and no J5
    account.
  </p>

  <h2>Usage data</h2>
  <p>
    The J5 Code server sends usage events to PostHog, an analytics service, so the maintainer can
    see how the software is used. Events are stored in the United States.
  </p>
  <p>The events are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the server starting, with a count of your threads and projects;</li>
    <li>
      a client connecting, with the kind of client (web, desktop, or mobile), its version, its
      operating system, the browser name on web, the device model and operating system version on
      mobile, and how it connected;
    </li>
    <li>a thread starting, and a turn being requested;</li>
    <li>
      a turn finishing, with the provider, model, reasoning effort, permission mode, result,
      duration, and token totals.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each event also carries the J5 Code version, the server's operating system and processor
    architecture, and the WSL distribution name if there is one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Events are tied to an identifier: a one-way hash of your Codex account ID, or of your Claude
    user ID if there is no Codex account, or of a random ID created on your machine if there is
    neither. The original ID is never sent. IP addresses are not stored.
  </p>
  <p>
    Events never include prompts, responses, file contents, file or project names, authentication
    tokens, or conversation IDs.
  </p>
  <p>
    To turn this off, set <code>T3CODE_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false</code> in the server's environment
    before starting it.
  </p>

  <h2>Other connections J5 Code makes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Update checks.</strong> J5 Code checks GitHub for new releases and downloads them
      from there. GitHub sees the request, under its own privacy policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your agent providers.</strong> When you run an agent, your prompts and code go to the
      provider you chose (such as OpenAI, Anthropic, or Cursor) through that provider's own tool,
      under your account and their terms. J5 Code's maintainer never receives them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The mobile app</h2>
  <p>
    The mobile app connects to J5 Code servers you add and stores those connection details on your
    device. It contains no analytics and sends nothing to the maintainer.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or requests about this page: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</Document>
